{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 583 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "349ad8540963ce5fcdbfa6cf388cb09cb1306484e841661c9450023f1d690f87" :edb/db-before-t 582 :edb/db-after-t 583 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533895 50 #inst "2026-10-05T09:15:36.714Z" 13194139533895 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264271 13194139533895 true] [17592186184092 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264278 13194139533895 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264264 13194139533895 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264285 13194139533895 true] [17592186184093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186264292 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264265 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1089 "q-117890" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/d54c590d8d64670c0fb90baea864d154cb4c59cd69d7f57234bfd8a5c4a3bfe7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1113 #uuid "6977be63-f7d9-5402-9e3e-ed4149021972" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264264 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+4C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x+4C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/336c38a44a3a40fcaf4a226f61a9f7af2cc731674c1ccc6679b80d846d4ea657.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore\n\n$$\n4C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264266 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264267 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264268 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264269 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264270 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1041 "selection" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264266 13194139533895 true] [17592186264265 1110 #uuid "00abf91d-e490-52e0-990b-e86391d300fa" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264266 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264266 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264266 1111 #uuid "3f8e3591-f142-5c76-b105-ed69de29e552" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264267 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264267 1111 #uuid "c1416dc0-034f-5156-9145-6ffddd35b063" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264268 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264268 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264268 1111 #uuid "5f99a275-8a84-5c4a-8449-1378c5866969" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264269 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264269 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264269 1111 #uuid "1a7453a2-6775-5dfa-ba33-6a9e13793dc9" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264270 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264270 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264270 1111 #uuid "d3700295-4eef-5aaa-a686-b739de422da6" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264272 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1089 "q-141999" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/8b237f9e857b1ed87de447aa79c14d359429e71d8afe7ab8eab201eba7ffb6af.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is  $B+2D?$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1113 #uuid "b85652aa-105e-56e5-bee4-742b27b9d838" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264271 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Therefore, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+2D=2+2\\cdot (1)=2+2=4.$  \n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)+1.\n$$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264273 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264274 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264275 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264276 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264277 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1041 "selection" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264274 13194139533895 true] [17592186264272 1110 #uuid "cd06d8d3-4017-5da2-b399-f066dc028bfa" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264273 1034 "2" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264273 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264273 1111 #uuid "0881f3e4-e1db-5b0d-91fe-b8b5e1a34b1e" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264274 1034 "4" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264274 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264274 1111 #uuid "119a7ffa-aa3f-59e9-985e-7361d5667cc3" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264275 1034 "0" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264275 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264275 1111 #uuid "9db5fa0a-5a66-50d6-9d73-ebf01c32d110" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264276 1034 "-1" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264276 1111 #uuid "69b40c59-c946-537e-9080-234210d2d2d9" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264277 1034 "-2" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264277 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264277 1111 #uuid "cdf87fd1-f795-5640-8860-ef91fcf2092e" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264279 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1089 "q-143666" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/148ef6b6d54262df159703437a6003e8f884052b98e95eb61536890a987a07f1.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1113 #uuid "9cef5111-5a4a-58fb-8dde-16bb9670ea94" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264278 1145 "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{9}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{9}{4}=-1.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264280 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264281 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264282 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264283 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264284 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1041 "selection" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264284 13194139533895 true] [17592186264279 1110 #uuid "a78e2d19-7dec-52f4-96d0-14d0ff65a95f" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264280 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264280 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264280 1111 #uuid "2cf54403-84cf-5b35-845a-8f068ff7067a" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264281 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264281 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264281 1111 #uuid "09a361bb-0e13-5df4-bd4e-5fe5f2ce04cf" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264282 1034 "1" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264282 1111 #uuid "1cecb0d4-8a59-5e36-a009-78c68c45cb83" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264283 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264283 1111 #uuid "78436634-6e1a-58d8-ac34-23b32dfebcdd" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264284 1034 "-1" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264284 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264284 1111 #uuid "5d759a0e-76f7-547c-bb28-27a23d3b0dfb" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264286 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1089 "q-143672" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/6a90f799d2dfd2dbd971e15eb195f4109ab7b6ceb204943a8ed2fd6c83bb620c.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1113 #uuid "2c0dbf5f-c695-5305-b340-7d3b320f640f" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264285 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=cot\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/0e94fbec60ae4d47d3a4fba50f8a875d9bb3938776940cc9688413d2cb22aa9e.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264287 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264288 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264289 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264290 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264291 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1041 "selection" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264291 13194139533895 true] [17592186264286 1110 #uuid "c52f41ed-d7f4-5ae3-9bd5-920d44dd7105" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264287 1034 "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264287 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264287 1111 #uuid "05d1cab4-a517-57e7-8455-7aea2ec8b78e" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264288 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{8}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264288 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264288 1111 #uuid "010d9895-c354-5b20-bc2f-e74894160f5e" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264289 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264289 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264289 1111 #uuid "ea6ac96a-4062-5f53-8a70-fc35e6b07148" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264290 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264290 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264290 1111 #uuid "ec72af4d-6d28-5d0a-88e1-91c144c71c3e" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264291 1034 "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264291 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264291 1111 #uuid "3bffd968-2c0a-51d6-a679-3ed68c880dfe" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1017 #edb/ref 17592186264293 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1089 "q-143676" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/0f994407d304e5b1105aeef37a62d6d51bcad43e76757846e0b42b8f257024b3.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1113 #uuid "6755a1e6-595a-542c-9f22-c873f4cb8de6" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264292 1145 "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{5C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{5}(x+\\frac{5C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13949463/assets/1b3e3365d8047befbe70c2371a44e61262c68a2da694ab410ae974917e8535bd.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{5})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5C}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,C=(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})(\\frac{2}{5})\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264294 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264295 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264296 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264297 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1000 #edb/ref 17592186264298 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1041 "selection" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1104 #edb/ref 17592186264295 13194139533895 true] [17592186264293 1110 #uuid "801996a9-63df-5cb7-9431-6c5a4a169b57" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264294 1034 "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264294 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264294 1111 #uuid "2a70298f-5e45-53e6-bc9e-3fff233e8071" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264295 1034 "-\\frac{\\pi }{5}" 13194139533895 true] [17592186264295 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533895 true] [17592186264295 1111 #uu